<h2>How to Write an RFP That Gets Accurate Quotes</h2>

<p>The biggest reason quotes vary 5x for the same project is that the brief is vague. Vague briefs force developers to assume the worst case, which inflates prices. A clear RFP saves you 20 to 40 percent on quotes. Include these sections in every brief:</p>

<ol>
    <li><strong>Business context.</strong> What does your company do? Who are your customers? Why do you need this website?</li>
    <li><strong>Goals and success metrics.</strong> Are you trying to rank in Google, capture leads, sell products, or onboard SaaS users? How will you measure success?</li>
    <li><strong>Scope.</strong> List every page or screen. List every integration. List every user role. The more specific, the more accurate the quote.</li>
    <li><strong>Design preferences.</strong> Three reference sites you love and three you hate, with reasons.</li>
    <li><strong>Tech preferences or constraints.</strong> Do you need to stay on WordPress? Do you have an existing API? Are there compliance requirements (HIPAA, GDPR, PCI)?</li>
    <li><strong>Timeline.</strong> When do you need to launch and why.</li>
    <li><strong>Budget range.</strong> Yes, share it. Hiding the budget wastes everyone's time and you will not get a lower price by hiding it.</li>
    <li><strong>Decision process.</strong> Who decides? How many quotes are you collecting? What is the timeline for picking a provider?</li>
</ol>

<p>A good developer reads that brief and either sends a confident fixed-price proposal within a week or asks five sharp follow-up questions. A bad developer sends an instant lowball with no detail. Filter accordingly.</p>

<h2>Red Flags in Cheap Website Quotes (And Why $500 Sites Cost More Long-Term)</h2>

<p>I have inherited dozens of "cheap" websites that ended up costing 5 to 10x to fix. Here are the red flags I look for:</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>No written proposal.</strong> If they will not put scope, deliverables, timeline, and payment terms in writing, walk away.</li>
    <li><strong>No portfolio of sites still running.</strong> Ask for three live URLs. Open them. Check Lighthouse scores. Check that they have not been replaced.</li>
    <li><strong>No discussion of hosting or maintenance.</strong> A developer who does not mention hosting or ongoing care is delivering you a problem, not a website.</li>
    <li><strong>Vague tech stack.</strong> "We use the latest technology" is not an answer. The right answer names specific frameworks and versions.</li>
    <li><strong>No mention of Core Web Vitals or Lighthouse performance score.</strong> Performance is non-negotiable in 2026. If they do not bring it up, they do not measure it.</li>
    <li><strong>100 percent payment upfront.</strong> Standard is 30 to 50 percent deposit, then milestone payments. Full upfront is a recipe for abandonment.</li>
    <li><strong>No references.</strong> Three past clients who will pick up the phone. Non-negotiable.</li>
</ul>

<p>A $500 website that breaks after three months and costs $3,000 to fix is more expensive than a $2,500 site that runs cleanly for five years. Cheap is the most expensive option in the long run.</p>

<h2>Fixed Price vs Hourly vs Retainer: Which Saves You Money</h2>

<p>Pricing models matter as much as the actual price. Here is when each one makes sense:</p>

<h3>Fixed Price</h3>

<p>Best for well-scoped projects where the deliverables are clear. The client knows exactly what they get and what they pay. The developer carries the risk of scope estimation. I quote fixed price when the spec is locked and the integrations are predictable.</p>

<p>The trap: any change requires a change order. If you are not sure what you want, fixed price will frustrate both sides.</p>

<h3>Hourly</h3>

<p>Best for ambiguous work, ongoing R&D, or projects where the scope evolves. The client pays for actual time. The developer is paid fairly for exploration. I use hourly when the client says "let's see how it goes."</p>

<p>The trap: bills can balloon. Always set a weekly cap and require weekly status reports with hour breakdowns.</p>

<h3>Retainer</h3>

<p>Best for ongoing relationships. The client buys a block of hours per month (typically 20 to 80) at a discounted rate. The developer commits priority availability. I run retainers with most of my recurring clients because both sides win: predictable income for me, faster turnaround and lower per-hour rate for them.</p>

<p>The trap: unused hours often do not roll over. Use them or lose them.</p>

<h3>Milestone-Based</h3>

<p>Hybrid of fixed price and hourly. The project is split into 3 to 6 milestones. Each milestone has its own scope, deliverable, and price. The client can pause or pivot between milestones without losing money. I default to this for any project longer than 8 weeks.</p>

<h2>How to Budget for a Website: A Step-by-Step Worksheet</h2>

<p>Here is the budget worksheet I walk every new client through. Fill in your numbers and you will have a realistic total within an hour:</p>

<ol>
    <li><strong>Pick your tier.</strong> Use the five tiers above and the description of your business to land on Tier 1 through 5.</li>
    <li><strong>Estimate build cost.</strong> Take the midpoint of the tier range. Tier 2 midpoint is $3,750.</li>
    <li><strong>Add content production.</strong> Budget 15 to 25 percent of build cost.</li>
    <li><strong>Add first-year hosting and tools.</strong> $300 to $3,000 depending on tier.</li>
    <li><strong>Add maintenance reserve.</strong> 10 to 20 percent of build cost annually.</li>
    <li><strong>Add a 15 percent contingency.</strong> Something will come up. Plan for it.</li>
    <li><strong>Subtract DIY portions.</strong> If you write your own copy or take your own photos, deduct that line item.</li>
</ol>

<p>For a Tier 2 custom marketing site, the math looks like: $3,750 build + $750 content + $400 hosting/tools + $500 maintenance + $800 contingency = roughly $6,200 first-year total. Subsequent years drop to about $1,500 to $2,000.</p>

<h2>When to DIY, When to Hire, and When to Wait</h2>

<p>Not every business needs a developer right now. Three honest scenarios:</p>

<h3>DIY Makes Sense When</h3>

<p>You are pre-revenue or validating an idea. You have under $1,500 to spend. You only need a one-page site. Use Carrd, Framer, or a Webflow template. You can ship in a weekend and learn what your real needs are.</p>

<h3>Hire Makes Sense When</h3>

<p>Your business depends on the website to make money. You have a budget of $2,500 or more. You value your time over your money. You need integrations, custom design, or anything beyond a brochure site.</p>

<h3>Wait Makes Sense When</h3>

<p>You have not validated demand. You are about to pivot your offering. You cannot articulate what the site should do. Build a free landing page, run a $200 ad test, and come back to the website conversation once you know what converts.</p>

<h2>ROI: When a Website Pays for Itself</h2>

<p>The cost of a website only matters relative to what it brings in. Quick math on payback periods:</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Local service business (plumber, dentist, gym):</strong> Average customer worth $500 lifetime. A $5,000 website that brings in 2 new customers per month pays for itself in 5 months.</li>
    <li><strong>E-commerce store:</strong> 50 percent gross margin, average order $80. A $12,000 store that does 30 orders per month pays for itself in 10 months.</li>
    <li><strong>B2B SaaS:</strong> Average ACV $1,200, gross margin 80 percent. A $25,000 MVP that lands 10 paying customers pays for itself in 3 months and starts compounding.</li>
    <li><strong>Content publisher:</strong> $20 CPM, 50,000 monthly pageviews after 6 months. A $4,000 site pays for itself in year one if you publish consistently.</li>
</ul>

<p>The websites that fail to pay for themselves are almost never the ones with bad design. They are the ones with bad distribution. Spend 60 percent of your effort on the website, 40 percent on getting traffic to it. If you reverse those percentages, you will lose money.</p>

<h2>How to Charge for a Website: Notes for Freelancers</h2>

<p>If you are reading this as a freelancer wondering how to charge for a website, the short answer is: pick a tier you can deliver excellently, set a price at the midpoint of the market for that tier in your geography, and raise prices 15 to 25 percent every year as your portfolio grows.</p>

<p>Here is a simplified Stripe checkout snippet that I include in almost every Tier 4 SaaS quote, just to show the kind of code a $20,000 project gets you:</p>

<pre><code>// app/Http/Controllers/BillingController.php
public function createCheckout(Request $request)
{
    $user = $request-&gt;user();

    $session = $user-&gt;newSubscription('pro', config('billing.price_pro'))
        -&gt;trialDays(14)
        -&gt;allowPromotionCodes()
        -&gt;checkout([
            'success_url' =&gt; route('billing.success'),
            'cancel_url'  =&gt; route('billing.cancel'),
        ]);

    return redirect($session-&gt;url);
}
</code></pre>

<p>And here is the kind of Next.js performance-tuned data fetching that ships on every Tier 2+ marketing site I deliver:</p>

<pre><code>// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export const revalidate = 3600;

export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await getAllPostSlugs();
  return posts.map((slug) =&gt; ({ slug }));
}

export default async function PostPage({ params }) {
  const post = await getPost(params.slug);
  if (!post) return notFound();

  return (
    &lt;article&gt;
      &lt;h1&gt;{post.title}&lt;/h1&gt;
      &lt;PostBody html={post.html} /&gt;
    &lt;/article&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>

<p>If your portfolio cannot show code at this level of care, you should be charging at the lower end of your tier. If it can, charge confidently at the upper end.</p>

<h2>The Modern Stack and Why It Affects Price</h2>

<p>In 2026 the stack you choose changes the price more than ever. A WordPress build uses well-known tools and a deep talent pool, which keeps prices stable. A modern stack with Next.js, a headless CMS, edge functions, and a database like PlanetScale or Neon costs 20 to 40 percent more to build but typically 30 to 50 percent less to run at scale.</p>

<p>If you want to dive into the trade-offs, I cover them in detail in <a href="/blog/web-development-trends-2026">web development trends 2026</a> and <a href="/blog/react-vs-vue-2026">react vs vue 2026</a>. For SaaS founders specifically, my walkthrough on how to <a href="/blog/build-saas-mvp-laravel-react-2026">build a SaaS MVP with Laravel and React in 2026</a> shows the exact stack I use for $15,000 to $30,000 budgets.</p>

<p>Performance affects price in another sneaky way. A slow site costs you money every month in lost conversions. I wrote a separate guide on <a href="/blog/why-your-website-loads-slowly">why your website loads slowly</a> with the most common causes. If your current site loads in over 3 seconds, fixing it is usually cheaper than rebuilding.</p>

<h2>Should You Build a Progressive Web App or a Regular Site?</h2>

<p>Progressive Web Apps add 15 to 30 percent to a build cost but can replace a native mobile app worth $30,000+. If your audience uses your site weekly on mobile, a PWA is a great investment. If they visit twice a year, skip it. I broke down the trade-offs in <a href="/blog/progressive-web-apps-2026">progressive web apps 2026</a>.</p>

<h2>API Design and Database Choices That Affect Price</h2>

<p>For SaaS projects, the cost of building the first version is small compared to the cost of redesigning a bad API or database six months later. Spending an extra $2,000 on proper API design and database modeling at the start saves $20,000 in refactoring later. Read my notes on <a href="/blog/api-design-best-practices-2026">api design best practices 2026</a> and <a href="/blog/database-design-for-web-apps">database design for web apps</a> before you sign any SaaS contract.</p>

<p>Similarly, optimizing Core Web Vitals from day one is much cheaper than retrofitting. The <a href="/blog/nextjs-performance-optimization-2026">Next.js performance optimization 2026</a> guide covers the patterns I use on every project.</p>

<h2>E-commerce-Specific Cost Considerations</h2>

<p>If you are building a store, the platform choice is the biggest cost lever. My full <a href="/blog/ecommerce-website-development-guide">ecommerce website development guide</a> walks through Shopify vs WooCommerce vs custom in detail. Quick rule: under 500 products and standard checkout, use Shopify. Over 500 products or custom B2B logic, consider WooCommerce or a custom stack. Over 10,000 products or international multi-warehouse, you are in Shopify Plus or custom territory and the budget jumps to $25,000+.</p>

<h2>A Realistic Example Deployment Script</h2>

<p>Clients often ask what they are paying for in the "DevOps" line item. Here is a stripped-down version of the deployment script I set up for almost every Tier 3 and Tier 4 project. It is the kind of automation that takes a developer two days to configure properly and saves the client weeks of downtime over the next five years:</p>

<pre><code>#!/bin/bash
set -e

echo "Pulling latest from main..."
git pull origin main

echo "Installing dependencies..."
composer install --no-dev --optimize-autoloader
npm ci &amp;&amp; npm run build

echo "Running migrations..."
php artisan migrate --force

echo "Clearing caches..."
php artisan config:cache
php artisan route:cache
php artisan view:cache

echo "Restarting queue workers..."
php artisan queue:restart

echo "Deployment complete."
</code></pre>

<p>That script is one of dozens of small pieces that separate a $3,000 build from a $15,000 build. The work is invisible to the client but visible in the uptime, the security, and the speed.</p>

<h2>Website Cost FAQs (2026 Edition)</h2>

<h3>How much does a basic website cost in 2026?</h3>
<p>A basic 5-page WordPress or Webflow site with a contact form and basic SEO costs $300 to $1,500 for a template build, or $1,500 to $4,000 for a custom design. Add $20 to $80 per month for hosting and updates. Most small businesses land around $2,500 to $4,000 total for year one.</p>

<h3>How much does it cost to build an e-commerce website in 2026?</h3>
<p>A Shopify store with a customized theme costs $3,000 to $8,000 for 50 to 500 products. A WooCommerce store with custom plugins costs $5,000 to $15,000. A fully custom Laravel or Node.js e-commerce platform starts at $15,000 and climbs to $50,000+ depending on catalog size, payment integrations, and inventory complexity.</p>

<h3>How much does a SaaS MVP cost in 2026?</h3>
<p>A serious SaaS MVP with authentication, multi-tenant data, Stripe billing, dashboards, and an admin panel costs $8,000 to $35,000 to build, typically over 8 to 16 weeks. Budget another $500 to $3,000 per month for ongoing infrastructure and development after launch.</p>

<h3>Why is there such a huge price difference between developers?</h3>
<p>Geography, experience, overhead, and scope clarity. A US agency charges $25,000 for what an Egyptian senior developer charges $6,000 because of cost of living and office overhead, not code quality. Vet on portfolio and references, not on price tag.</p>

<h3>How much should I budget for website maintenance per year?</h3>
<p>Plan for 10 to 20 percent of build cost per year for maintenance. A $5,000 site needs $500 to $1,000 per year for hosting, plugin updates, security monitoring, and small content edits. A $50,000 SaaS needs $5,000 to $10,000 annually for the same plus security patches and feature work.</p>

<h3>Is it cheaper to use WordPress, Webflow, or custom code?</h3>
<p>WordPress is the cheapest entry point ($200 to $1,500 first year DIY) but needs ongoing maintenance. Webflow is mid-range ($400 to $2,000 first year) with no maintenance hassle but ongoing platform fees. Custom code starts at $4,000+ and scales the best, but you need a developer for every change. Pick based on your team's skills and growth plans, not just upfront price.</p>

<h3>What hidden costs should I plan for beyond the build quote?</h3>
<p>Content production ($1,000 to $10,000), SaaS subscriptions ($50 to $500/month), compliance and legal documents ($200 to $5,000), translations if you go multilingual, hosting and domain, and a 15 percent contingency reserve. Real first-year cost is usually 25 to 40 percent above the build quote.</p>

<h3>Should I pay fixed price, hourly, or retainer?</h3>
<p>Fixed price for well-scoped projects where deliverables are clear. Hourly for ambiguous R&D work. Retainer for ongoing relationships where you need 20+ hours of development per month. Milestone-based is the safest hybrid for any project over 8 weeks.</p>

<h2>Final Word: How to Get an Honest Quote</h2>

<p>The single most useful thing you can do before spending money on a website in 2026 is to write a clear brief, get three honest quotes from developers in different geographies, and ask each one to explain their assumptions. The right developer will ask sharp questions back. The wrong one will just send a number.</p>

<p>If you want a free 30-minute consultation where I review your goals, recommend a realistic tier and budget, and tell you honestly whether you should hire me, hire someone cheaper, or wait six months, head to my <a href="/contact">contact page</a> and book a call. I have shipped 25+ production projects across seven countries and I will tell you the truth even if it costs me the project. That is the only way I know how to do this work.</p>
